;;; Transform.mon --- Generic sequence transformations with static evidence

:author   Laluxx
:version  0.6.0
:keywords sequence transform producer fusion evidence flat-map scan rate

;;; Commentary:

 Transform owns representation-changing sequence operations. Every operation is
 written once over Sequence and preserves the source Evaluation strategy unless
 its signature explicitly proves a strategy relation.

 The runtime representation is still one existential Producer. Transform does
 not define MapView, FilterView, FlatMapView, or strict/lazy variants. A single
 View wrapper stores the Producer while erased phantom indices retain only the
 semantic provenance needed by the proof layer.

;;; Evidence Profiles, Not Operation Tags

 Earlier cycles used one phantom tag per operation. That was safe but too close
 to recording implementation history. Several transformations have exactly the
 same productivity/finiteness behavior and therefore deserve one proof profile.

 The promoted Transform values in this cycle classify preservation behavior:

   PreservingTransform
     productive -> productive, finite -> finite, infinite -> infinite.
     map, finite drop, stateful map, scan, enumerate, and intersperse belong here.

   BoundedTransform
     productive -> finite. Used by take with a finite Int bound.

   PrefixTransform
     productive -> productive and finite -> finite, but an infinite source may
     terminate early. Used by take-while.

   StarvingTransform
     only a finite source generically proves productivity/finiteness. Filter and
     drop-while may inspect infinitely many productive source values before their
     next visible output.

 Append, Zip, and FlatMap retain separate profiles because their theorems depend
 on more than one source.

 This is deliberately an evidence algebra rather than an operation enumeration.
 Adding another one-to-one stateful transform should reuse PreservingTransform,
 not add another constructor merely to name the function.

;;; Strategy-Locked Binary Composition

 Core does not yet expose a source-level equality constraint spelling such as
 =Strategy l ~ Strategy r=. Binary and nested Producer combinators nevertheless
 require exactly one Evaluation parameter. This cycle therefore exposes lowered
 forms whose right/nested inputs are explicitly =Producer (Strategy left) ...=.
 The producer type itself is the witness that evaluation strategies agree; for
 append it also fixes the visible element type.

 These lowered forms are the semantically complete boundary when callers combine
 different Sequence state types. The higher-level convenience functions remain
 for cases where elaboration can infer the same equalities from =lower right=,
 and now delegate through the strategy-locked implementation rather than owning
 a second composition path. No proof certificate is synthesized merely from a
 Producer value: productivity/finiteness evidence remains a separate theorem.

;;; Rates, Nesting, and Stateful Mapping

 Producer Step has three raw rates: Stop, Skip, and Yield. Flat-map composes those
 steps so one outer Yield may lead to zero, one, or arbitrarily many visible
 outputs over time. The outer continuation remains suspended while an inner
 producer is live.

 Stateful mapping is represented by map-accumulate. Its state is private producer
 state, not externally observable mutation. Scan and enumerate are ordinary
 specializations. Intersperse is a small buffered machine that exercises a rate
 greater than one without inventing a second sequence representation.

 These operators are intentionally implemented in Producer rather than as new
 Sequence classes. They stress the same calculus that filter/zip already use and
 provide the reference semantics against which compiler normalization is proved.

;;; Complete-Fusion Boundary

 Core guarantees semantic fusion: a nested transform pipeline contains no
 materialized intermediate Sequence and lowers to one Producer calculus.

 Zero-overhead representation fusion is stronger. In particular flat-map stores
 an existential inner Producer chosen by a callback. The compiler must normalize
 such statically known producer compositions by closure conversion,
 defunctionalization, state-product simplification, and constructor elimination.
 It must not rely on accidental inlining.

 This distinction follows complete-fusion work, which shows that nesting, zip,
 and differing rates require an explicit normalization theory. A first-class
 Producer or callback that escapes dynamically remains a legitimate runtime
 value; complete representation erasure is promised only after the relevant
 pipeline structure becomes static through specialization.

;;; Research Basis

 Duncan Coutts, Roman Leshchinskiy, and Don Stewart, "Stream Fusion: From Lists
 to Streams to Nothing at All", ICFP 2007.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/1291151.1291199

 Oleg Kiselyov, Aggelos Biboudis, Nick Palladinos, and Yannis Smaragdakis,
 "Stream Fusion, to Completeness", POPL 2017.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/3009837.3009880

 Oleg Kiselyov, Tomoaki Kobayashi, and Nick Palladinos, "Complete Fusion for
 Stateful Streams: Equational Theory of Stateful Streams and Fusion as
 Normalization-by-Evaluation", arXiv:2412.15768, revised 2026.
   https://arxiv.org/abs/2412.15768

 Tomoaki Kobayashi and Oleg Kiselyov, "Complete Stream Fusion for
 Software-Defined Radio", PEPM 2024.
   https://doi.org/10.1145/3635800.3636962

 The 2017 work isolates nesting and rate differences as central obstacles to
 complete fusion. The 2026 revision develops a unique-normal-form/NBE account for
 stateful streams, while the SDR case study demonstrates that map-accumulate and
 window-like processing can be expressed without multiplying stream shapes.

;;; Code:

import Control.Evaluation
import Data.Bool
import Data.Functor
import Data.Sequence.Producer
import Sequence

module Data.Sequence.Transform
  [Transform PreservingTransform BoundedTransform PrefixTransform
   StarvingTransform AppendTransform ZipTransform FlatMapTransform
   View sequence-map sequence-filter sequence-take sequence-drop
   sequence-take-while sequence-drop-while
   sequence-map-accumulate sequence-map-filter-accumulate
   sequence-scan sequence-enumerate sequence-intersperse
   sequence-append-lowered sequence-zip-lowered sequence-zip-with-lowered sequence-flat-map-lowered
   sequence-append sequence-zip sequence-zip-with sequence-flat-map]


;;; Static Evidence Profiles


data Transform :: Type
  PreservingTransform : Transform
  BoundedTransform    : Transform
  PrefixTransform     : Transform
  StarvingTransform   : Transform
  AppendTransform     : Transform
  ZipTransform        : Transform
  FlatMapTransform    : Transform


;;; Generic View


data View :: Transform -> Type -> (Type -> Type) -> Type -> Type
  ViewValue : Producer e a -> View proof source e a


instance Functor e => Functor (View proof source e)
  map f [ViewValue source] -> ViewValue (map f source)


instance Evaluation e => Sequence (View proof source e a)
  type Element (View proof source e a) = a
  type Strategy (View proof source e a) = e

  uncons [ViewValue source] -> uncons source
  lower [ViewValue source]  -> source


;;; Preservation Theorems


instance ProductiveSequence s =>
  ProductiveSequence (View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance FiniteSequence s =>
  FiniteSequence (View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance InfiniteSequence s =>
  InfiniteSequence (View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance ProductiveSequence s =>
  ProductiveSequence (View BoundedTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance ProductiveSequence s =>
  FiniteSequence (View BoundedTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance ProductiveSequence s =>
  ProductiveSequence (View PrefixTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance FiniteSequence s =>
  FiniteSequence (View PrefixTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance FiniteSequence s =>
  ProductiveSequence (View StarvingTransform s (Strategy s) b)


instance FiniteSequence s =>
  FiniteSequence (View StarvingTransform s (Strategy s) b)


;; Multi-source profiles state only propositions justified by all sources they
;; may need to observe. These instances are intentionally conservative: absence
;; of a certificate is preferable to a certificate with an insufficient premise.

instance (ProductiveSequence l, ProductiveSequence r) =>
  ProductiveSequence (View AppendTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) b)


instance (FiniteSequence l, FiniteSequence r) =>
  FiniteSequence (View AppendTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) b)


;; An infinite left append never reaches the right source.
instance InfiniteSequence l =>
  InfiniteSequence (View AppendTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) b)


instance (ProductiveSequence l, ProductiveSequence r) =>
  ProductiveSequence (View ZipTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) c)


;; Zip is left-to-right. A finite left source bounds the number of right
;; observations, while productivity of the right source makes those demands
;; terminate. The symmetric theorem can be added once overlapping evidence
;; resolution has an explicit coherence rule.
instance (FiniteSequence l, ProductiveSequence r) =>
  FiniteSequence (View ZipTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) c)


instance (InfiniteSequence l, InfiniteSequence r) =>
  InfiniteSequence (View ZipTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) c)


;; Flat-map may starve on infinitely many empty inner sequences. A finite outer
;; source plus productive inners is enough for productivity; finiteness needs
;; both levels finite. Infinitude is certified only by the strong case where an
;; infinite outer yields an infinite inner type at its first visible element.
instance (FiniteSequence outer, ProductiveSequence inner) =>
  ProductiveSequence (View FlatMapTransform (outer, inner) (Strategy outer) b)


instance (FiniteSequence outer, FiniteSequence inner) =>
  FiniteSequence (View FlatMapTransform (outer, inner) (Strategy outer) b)


instance (InfiniteSequence outer, InfiniteSequence inner) =>
  InfiniteSequence (View FlatMapTransform (outer, inner) (Strategy outer) b)


;;; Transformations


;; One-to-one and finite-rate preserving transforms share one evidence profile.
define sequence-map :: Sequence s =>
  (Element s -> b) -> s -> View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) b
  f values -> ViewValue (map f (lower values))


define sequence-drop :: Sequence s =>
  Int -> s -> View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) (Element s)
  amount values -> ViewValue (drop-producer amount (lower values))


define sequence-map-accumulate :: Sequence s =>
  (state -> Element s -> (state, b)) -> state -> s
  -> View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) b
  combine initial values ->
    ViewValue (map-accumulate-producer combine initial (lower values))


define sequence-map-filter-accumulate :: Sequence s =>
  (state -> Element s -> MapFilterResult state b) -> state -> s
  -> View StarvingTransform s (Strategy s) b
  combine initial values ->
    ViewValue (map-filter-accumulate-producer combine initial (lower values))


define sequence-scan :: Sequence s =>
  (state -> Element s -> state) -> state -> s
  -> View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) state
  combine initial values ->
    ViewValue (scan-producer combine initial (lower values))


define sequence-enumerate :: Sequence s =>
  s -> View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) (Int, Element s)
  values -> ViewValue (enumerate-producer (lower values))


define sequence-intersperse :: Sequence s =>
  Element s -> s -> View PreservingTransform s (Strategy s) (Element s)
  separator values ->
    ViewValue (intersperse-producer separator (lower values))


;; Bounded and potentially starving transforms have distinct proof profiles.
define sequence-take :: Sequence s =>
  Int -> s -> View BoundedTransform s (Strategy s) (Element s)
  amount values -> ViewValue (take-producer amount (lower values))


define sequence-take-while :: Sequence s =>
  (Element s -> Bool) -> s
  -> View PrefixTransform s (Strategy s) (Element s)
  predicate values ->
    ViewValue (take-while-producer predicate (lower values))


define sequence-filter :: Sequence s =>
  (Element s -> Bool) -> s
  -> View StarvingTransform s (Strategy s) (Element s)
  predicate values ->
    ViewValue (filter-producer predicate (lower values))


define sequence-drop-while :: Sequence s =>
  (Element s -> Bool) -> s
  -> View StarvingTransform s (Strategy s) (Element s)
  predicate values ->
    ViewValue (drop-while-producer predicate (lower values))


;; Strategy-locked lowered composition. These signatures state the common
;; Evaluation parameter directly even before the language has a type-equality
;; constraint syntax for associated Strategy families.
define sequence-append-lowered :: Sequence l =>
  l -> Producer (Strategy l) (Element l) -> Producer (Strategy l) (Element l)
  left right -> append-producer (lower left) right


define sequence-zip-lowered :: Sequence l =>
  l -> Producer (Strategy l) b -> Producer (Strategy l) (Element l, b)
  left right -> zip-producer (lower left) right


define sequence-zip-with-lowered :: Sequence l =>
  (Element l -> b -> c) -> l -> Producer (Strategy l) b
  -> Producer (Strategy l) c
  combine left right -> zip-with-producer combine (lower left) right


define sequence-flat-map-lowered :: Sequence outer =>
  (Element outer -> Producer (Strategy outer) b) -> outer
  -> Producer (Strategy outer) b
  expand values -> flat-map-producer expand (lower values)


;; Binary and nested convenience transforms use the same strategy-locked path.
define sequence-append ::
  (Sequence l, Sequence r) =>
  l -> r -> View AppendTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) (Element l)
  left right ->
    ViewValue (sequence-append-lowered left (lower right))


define sequence-zip ::
  (Sequence l, Sequence r) =>
  l -> r -> View ZipTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) (Element l, Element r)
  left right ->
    ViewValue (sequence-zip-lowered left (lower right))


define sequence-zip-with ::
  (Sequence l, Sequence r) =>
  (Element l -> Element r -> c) -> l -> r
  -> View ZipTransform (l, r) (Strategy l) c
  combine left right ->
    ViewValue (sequence-zip-with-lowered combine left (lower right))


define sequence-flat-map ::
  (Sequence outer, Sequence inner) =>
  (Element outer -> inner) -> outer
  -> View FlatMapTransform (outer, inner) (Strategy outer) (Element inner)
  expand values ->
    ViewValue
      (sequence-flat-map-lowered
        (lambda (value) (lower (expand value)))
        values)


;;; Small Observational Tests


define transform-first-or :: a -> (a, e s)? -> a
  fallback nil -> fallback
  _ (value, _) -> value


define transform-empty? :: (a, e s)? -> Bool
  nil -> True
  (_, _) -> False


define duplicate-value :: Int -> [Int]
  value -> list value value


define running-sum :: Int -> Int -> Int
  total value -> total + value


define alternate-output :: Bool -> Int -> MapFilterResult Bool Int
  False _     -> Filtered True
  True value  -> Emitted False value


tests
  :generic:
  assert-eq
    (transform-first-or 0
      (uncons (sequence-map (lambda (x) (+ x 1)) (list 1 2 3))))
    2
    "map transforms arbitrary Sequence states"
  assert-eq
    (transform-first-or 0
      (uncons (sequence-filter (lambda (x) (> x 1)) (list 1 2 3))))
    2
    "filter transforms through one producer machine"
  assert-eq
    (transform-empty? (uncons (sequence-take 0 (list 1 2 3))))
    True
    "take zero terminates without a visible element"
  assert-eq
    (transform-first-or 0
      (uncons
        (sequence-map (lambda (x) (+ x 10))
          (sequence-filter (lambda (x) (> x 2))
            (sequence-drop 1 (list 1 2 3))))))
    13
    "nested transformations remain one lowered producer pipeline"
  assert-eq
    (transform-first-or 0
      (uncons (sequence-flat-map duplicate-value (list 3 4))))
    3
    "flat-map enters the first inner producer without materializing a sequence"
  assert-eq
    (transform-first-or (0, 0)
      (uncons (sequence-zip-lowered (list 1 2) (lower (list 9 8)))))
    (1, 9)
    "lowered zip carries common Evaluation strategy in its Producer type"
  assert-eq
    (transform-first-or 99
      (uncons (sequence-scan running-sum 0 (list 1 2 3))))
    0
    "scan emits its seed before observing the source"
  assert-eq
    (transform-first-or 0
      (uncons
        (sequence-map-filter-accumulate
          alternate-output False (list 1 2 3 4))))
    2
    "stateful zero-or-one transforms skip without materialization"
  :generic:
